   "id": "k2-59",
   "name": "Electrolytes and non-electrolytes"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "An electrolyte is a compound that conducts electricity when molten or dissolved in water, and is decomposed by it. It contains ions.\n• Strong electrolytes: solutions of salts (sodium chloride, copper(II) sulphate), strong acids (hydrochloric acid, sulphuric acid) and strong alkalis (sodium hydroxide); molten salts.\n• Weak electrolytes: vinegar (ethanoic acid), lime juice, ammonia solution: the bulb glows dimly.\nA non-electrolyte does not conduct, in any state: sugar solution, ethanol (alcohol), kerosene, distilled water, urea solution.\nElectrolytes in the body: blood contains sodium, potassium and chloride ions; oral rehydration solution (ORS) replaces lost electrolytes after diarrhoea.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "A bulb lights brightly with salt solution, dimly with vinegar and not at all with sugar solution. Classify each liquid.",
  "steps": [
   "Bright: many ions — a strong electrolyte (salt solution).",
   "Dim: few ions — a weak electrolyte (vinegar). No light: no ions — a non-electrolyte (sugar solution)."
  ],
  "answer": "Salt solution: strong; vinegar: weak; sugar solution: non-electrolyte.",
  "checks": []
 },
